<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Delos</h1><p class="st-meta">314 BCE – 167 BCE · capital Delos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The sacred island of Apollo, independent from 314 to 167 BCE, when its sanctuary became a meeting place of kings and a hub of Aegean commerce.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Delos, birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artemis">Artemis</a> in Greek myth, had long been under Athenian control. In 314 BCE, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a> I extending his power into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, the Athenians were ousted and the Delians governed their island as an independent city, also hosting the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nesiotic-league">Nesiotic League</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Independent Delos was run by an archon, council and assembly, while elected hieropoioi managed the sanctuary's lands, loans and treasures; their yearly inscribed accounts are among the richest economic records from antiquity. Kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a> built porticoes and dedications. Banking and trade grew, drawing merchants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the East.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 167 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> handed Delos to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> and made it a free port to damage <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes</a>; the Delians were expelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>. Delos then boomed as a slave and grain market until sacked in 88 BCE by the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI</a> and again by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> in 69 BCE. It is now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/delos.jpg" alt="Delos" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Romain Delanoë · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ruines_de_D%C3%A9los.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>314 BCE – 167 BCE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Delos</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancient Greek religion (cult of Apollo)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Delian sanctuary</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-240&amp;focus=delos" aria-label="Delos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5312 832 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Delos lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5632 1088L5632 1088M5632 1088L5591 1068"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5632 1088h0M5591 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5632 1088h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5632 1088h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-240&amp;focus=delos">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
